---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import register from '../data/register.json';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
const fam = register.people.filter(p => p.kind === 'family');
const idx = register.people.filter(p => p.kind === 'index');
const c = register.counts;
---
<Base title="The register"
  subtitle={c.published + ' of ' + c.total + ' people in the source, published'}
  lede="Everyone the source records, with the two kinds of entry kept firmly apart: the family proper, and a hundred-odd people who share the surname and have not been shown to share anything else.">

  <div class="cards">
    <div class="card stat"><b>{c.family}</b><span>Family</span></div>
    <div class="card stat"><b>{c.index}</b><span>Surname index</span></div>
    <div class="card stat"><b>{c.omitted_living}</b><span>Living, omitted</span></div>
    <div class="card stat"><b>{c.total}</b><span>In the source</span></div>
  </div>

  <h2>The family</h2>
  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Born</th><th>Died</th><th>Noted</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {fam.map(p => (
        <tr>
          <td><strong>{p.name}</strong>{p.bare && <> <span class="tag inf">Living — named only</span></>}</td>
          <td>{p.born || (p.by ? String(p.by) : '')}</td>
          <td>{p.no_death_recorded ? <span class="muted small">no death recorded</span> : p.died}</td>
          <td class="small muted">{(p.facts || []).filter(f => f.place || f.c).slice(0, 2).map(f => (f.t + (f.place ? ': ' + f.place : '') + (f.c ? ' — ' + f.c : ''))).join(' · ').slice(0, 150)}</td>
        </tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
  </table></div>

  <h2>The surname index — not ancestry</h2>
  <div class="panel">
    <p>
      The source tree hangs about a hundred people off two nodes literally named
      <em>“UNKNOWN PLACEHOLDERS Luwinski”</em> and <em>“Germany Luwinski”</em>. They run from 1757 to
      1929 and carry Polish spouse surnames — Grzegorzewska, Kruczyńska, Paturalska, Sabędzka,
      Błożkiewicz, Leciejewska, Melkowa, Klich.
    </p>
    <p>
      That is a <strong>collection of every Luwinski found in the German and Polish registers</strong>,
      parked under a placeholder so it could be kept. It is useful and worth keeping. It is not descent,
      and it is not presented here as though it were. Not one of these people has been shown to connect
      to Joachim Luwinski of Allenstein. <span class="tag disp">Unproven</span>
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>And this is where the photographs are <span class="tag disp">Unproven</span></h3>
    <p>
      Opening every image in the source tree produced seven identified faces. Two are
      <strong>Georg Leibholz</strong>, murdered at Chełmno — the only picture in the archive of anyone
      who was killed. The other five are all one family: <strong>Josef Fuhl</strong> (b. 1876) and
      <strong>Martha Fuhl née Schulz</strong> (b. 1874) of Berlin, their daughter <strong>Helene
      Auguste</strong> (1905–1977) in a cloche hat, her husband <strong>Josef Turowski</strong>
      (1902–1963) in a stiff collar, and their daughter <strong>Helene Maria Frubrich née
      Turowski</strong> (1930–2020).
    </p>
    <p>
      They reach this tree because another Fuhl daughter, <strong>Martha</strong> (b. 14 September
      1903), married <strong>Leo Luwinski</strong>, born 22 March 1902. And Leo's father, in the source
      tree, is <em>“Germany Luwinski”</em> — the placeholder above. He is given twenty-four brothers
      whose dates run from 1807 to 1923.
    </p>
    <p>
      So the archive now has faces, and they are ordinary ones: a wedding hat, a suit, a woman grown old
      in a garden. <strong>But they are faces from the index, not from the line.</strong> Nothing
      connects Leo Luwinski to Joachim Luwinski of Allenstein except the surname, and until something
      does, these are photographs of a family that may be no relation at all. They are named here and
      not shown.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Born</th><th>Died</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {idx.map(p => (<tr><td>{p.name}</td><td>{p.born}</td><td>{p.died}</td></tr>))}
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
</Base>
